The frequency itself is a useful bucket index. A character appearing `f` times goes into bucket `f`. Walking the buckets from the largest index down puts the most frequent characters first.

The returned value uses the required `vector<char>` template. The input string is only read and is not changed.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<char> frequencySort(string& s) {
        unordered_map<char, int> counts;
        for (char ch : s) {
            counts[ch]++;
        }

        vector<vector<char>> buckets(s.size() + 1);
        for (const auto& entry : counts) {
            buckets[entry.second].push_back(entry.first);
        }

        vector<char> result;
        result.reserve(s.size());

        for (int frequency = static_cast<int>(s.size()); frequency >= 1; frequency--) {
            for (char ch : buckets[frequency]) {
                for (int i = 0; i < frequency; i++) {
                    result.push_back(ch);
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

For an empty string, the bucket list has one empty bucket and the loop adds nothing, so an empty result is returned.

Time complexity: `O(n + k)`, where `n` is the string length and `k` is the number of different characters.

Space complexity: `O(n + k)` for the buckets and result.

A small count map is enough here. After counting each character, sort the `(character, count)` pairs from the largest count to the smallest, then repeat each character from the sorted list. Characters with the same count may appear in either order.

For `s = "tree"`, the counts are `e: 2`, `t: 1`, and `r: 1`, so the result can be `"eetr"` or another valid order for the tied characters.

```python
class Solution:
    def frequencySort(self, s):
        counts = {}

        for ch in s:
            counts[ch] = counts.get(ch, 0) + 1

        ordered = sorted(counts.items(), key=lambda item: item[1], reverse=True)

        result = []
        for ch, count in ordered:
            result.append(ch * count)

        return ''.join(result)
```

Time complexity: `O(n + k log k)`, where `n` is the string length and `k` is the number of different characters.

Space complexity: `O(n + k)` including the returned string.

A max heap avoids sorting the original string. First, the map records counts. Then each map entry goes into a priority queue ordered by count. Removing entries from the queue gives the output order directly.

Dry run with `"aabbc"`: the heap first removes `a` and `b`, both with count 2, followed by `c` with count 1. Either order for `a` and `b` is valid because their counts match.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public List<Character> frequencySort(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();

        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {
            counts.put(ch, counts.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);
        }

        PriorityQueue<Map.Entry<Character, Integer>> maxHeap =
            new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(b.getValue(), a.getValue()));

        maxHeap.addAll(counts.entrySet());

        List<Character> result = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!maxHeap.isEmpty()) {
            Map.Entry<Character, Integer> entry = maxHeap.poll();
            for (int i = 0; i < entry.getValue(); i++) {
                result.add(entry.getKey());
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n + k log k)`, counting and building the result take `O(n)`, while heap operations use `O(k log k)`.

Space complexity: `O(n + k)` including the result list.

This version uses a frequency dictionary, then repeatedly searches for the character with the largest remaining count. Once that character is added, its count is removed from the dictionary so it cannot be selected again.

It is easy to follow, but it scans all remaining characters again for every group. That makes it slower than a heap or bucket method when many different characters are present.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Solution
{
    public List<char> FrequencySort(string s)
    {
        Dictionary<char, int> counts = new Dictionary<char, int>();

        foreach (char ch in s)
        {
            if (!counts.ContainsKey(ch))
            {
                counts[ch] = 0;
            }

            counts[ch]++;
        }

        List<char> result = new List<char>(s.Length);

        while (counts.Count > 0)
        {
            char bestCharacter = '\0';
            int bestCount = -1;

            foreach (KeyValuePair<char, int> entry in counts)
            {
                if (entry.Value > bestCount)
                {
                    bestCount = entry.Value;
                    bestCharacter = entry.Key;
                }
            }

            for (int i = 0; i < bestCount; i++)
            {
                result.Add(bestCharacter);
            }

            counts.Remove(bestCharacter);
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

The empty string is handled because the dictionary starts empty and the selection loop is skipped.

Time complexity: `O(n + k^2)`, where `k` is the number of different characters.

Space complexity: `O(n + k)` including the result list.

The map is used only for counting. After that, the distinct characters are copied into a slice of pairs and sorted by decreasing frequency. Building the answer from the sorted pairs keeps each character together instead of sorting individual runes.

For `"cccaaab"`, the pairs are ordered as `a: 3`, `c: 3`, and `b: 1`. The two groups with count 3 can be returned in either order.

```go
package main

import "sort"

type characterCount struct {
    character rune
    count     int
}

func frequencySort(s string) []rune {
    counts := make(map[rune]int)
    for _, ch := range s {
        counts[ch]++
    }

    pairs := make([]characterCount, 0, len(counts))
    for ch, count := range counts {
        pairs = append(pairs, characterCount{
            character: ch,
            count:     count,
        })
    }

    sort.Slice(pairs, func(i, j int) bool {
        return pairs[i].count > pairs[j].count
    })

    result := make([]rune, 0, len([]rune(s)))
    for _, pair := range pairs {
        for i := 0; i < pair.count; i++ {
            result = append(result, pair.character)
        }
    }

    return result
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n + k log k)`, with `k` distinct runes.

Space complexity: `O(n + k)` including the returned rune slice.

## ✅ Approach
1. Create a hash array of 26 pairs.
   - `first` stores the frequency.
   - `second` stores the corresponding character (`'a'` to `'z'`).

2. Traverse the string once and count the frequency of each character.

3. Sort the array of pairs using a custom comparator:
   - Higher frequency comes first.
   - If two characters have the same frequency, the lexicographically smaller character comes first.

4. Traverse the sorted array and add only those characters whose frequency is greater than `0`.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{	
    private:
        static bool internal_comparator(pair<int,char> & p1, pair<int,char> & p2){
            // freq comparison
            if(p1.first > p2.first){
                return true;
            }
            if(p1.first < p2.first){
                return false;
            }

            // if freq is same
            if(p1.second > p2.second){
                return false;
            }
            return true;
        }
	public:
		vector<char> frequencySort(string& s){

            // hash pair
            pair<int, char> freq[26];

            for(int i = 0; i<26; i++){      // adding a-z with 0 freq.
                freq[i] = {0, i + 'a'};
            }

            // pre-compute
            for(int i = 0; i<s.size(); i++){
                freq[s[i] - 'a'].first++;   // adding the freq.
            }

            // sort the hash pair 
            sort(freq, freq + 26, internal_comparator);

            // empty char array to store answer
            vector<char> vec;

            // pushing the elements whose freq is greater than 0
            for(int i = 0; i<26; i++){
                if(freq[i].first == 0) break;
                vec.push_back(freq[i].second);
            }

            return vec;

		}
};
```

